WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.009
How robust can any classifier possibly be against
an adversary that perturbs inputs at test time?

00:00:06.089 --> 00:00:09.839
This paper answers that question for the
multi-class setting.

00:00:09.919 --> 00:00:15.300
The authors derive achievable information-theoretic
lower bounds on the 0-1 loss of the

00:00:15.380 --> 00:00:18.565
best possible classifier under a test-time attacker,

00:00:18.645 --> 00:00:20.134
for any discrete dataset.

00:00:20.214 --> 00:00:25.784
Their framework builds a conflict hypergraph from
the data and the attacker's constraints,

00:00:25.864 --> 00:00:31.309
then solves a linear program whose optimum is the
lowest loss any classifier can attain.

00:00:31.389 --> 00:00:34.698
Because the exact problem is often too large to solve,

00:00:34.778 --> 00:00:39.470
they introduce efficient bounds that pin down the
range of the optimal loss,

00:00:39.550 --> 00:00:44.868
and use them to reveal a striking gap between
today's adversarially trained models and

00:00:44.948 --> 00:00:46.940
what is theoretically achievable.
